From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 14:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] watchdog: sunxi: A733: add WDT0 to the DT
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add the A733's WDT0 to the board DT via a fallback compatible on the
A523 watchdog, and extend the binding to accept that compatible. The
block is real hardware and belongs in the DT on its own merits: it gives
the board a working /dev/watchdog.

THE CARRY THIS PATCH USED TO CARRY, AND WHY IT IS GONE (2026-08-08)

Until now this patch also added an optional `priority` DT property to
`sunxi_wdt`, set it to 192 in the node, and existed mainly for that: the
prebuilt vendor BL31 blob (`awbin/a733/bl31.bin`,
`v2.5(debug):177ce84c2-dirty`) had a PSCI SYSTEM_RESET that never
completed, PSCI's restart handler registers at 129 against the
watchdog's 128, and `kernel/reboot.c` walks the chain in descending
priority -- so `reboot` reached PSCI, hung there, and never reached the
watchdog. Raising the watchdog above 129 was the workaround.

Its own drop condition, quoted from the version of this header that
shipped: "a boot of a chain whose BL31 was built from source, in which
`reboot` resets the board with the `priority = <192>;` line deleted from
the watchdog node. At that point delete the line (and, if nothing else
has grown a use for it, the driver hunk and the binding's `priority`
property with it). Keep the node."

That observation exists. On 2026-07-20, on the source-BL31 branch, `reboot -f` reached
`BOOT0 is starting!` on a tree that carried **no version of this patch at all** --
not the line deleted, the whole patch absent -- and with U-Boot's
watchdog not started either (`WDT: Not starting watchdog@2050000`). The
condition is met more strongly than it was written. The source chain has
since landed and is what ships (HW-proven 2026-08-03).

The mechanism, so nobody reads this as "PSCI suddenly works": TF-A v2.12
builds `plat/allwinner/sun60i_a733` with `STANDALONE_WATCHDOG := 1`, and
`sunxi_native_pm.c` therefore implements SYSTEM_RESET as a single
`mmio_write_32(SUNXI_R_WDOG_BASE + 0x0008, 0x16aa0001)` -- a watchdog
soft reset **inside BL31**. The reset never leaves firmware. The v2.5
blob did not do this, which is the whole difference.
(This describes the TF-A v2.12 tree of 2026-08. The recipe now builds TF-A
master with the TF-A patch `allwinner-system-reset-through-a-standalone-watchdog`,
which also keeps SYSTEM_RESET inside BL31.)

So all three carry pieces are removed here: the `priority = <192>;` line,
the driver hunk in `sunxi_wdt.c`, and the binding's `priority` property
plus its `$ref` to `restart-handler.yaml#`. Nothing else in this tree
reads `priority` (checked: it appears in no other patch). What stays is
the node and its compatible.

WHERE EACH VALUE COMES FROM

Spec: A733 UM 8.3.5 places WDT0_CPUX at 0x02050000; the documented defaults are the last row below.

Silicon -- register readback, `/sys/class/sunxi_dump`, 2026-07-22, read
on both boards, 0x02050000..0x02050020:

              +0x00  +0x04  +0x08  +0x0c  +0x10  +0x14  +0x18
  first 6 GiB   0      0      0      0      1      0xb1   0x1f
  second 6 GiB  0      0      0      0      1      0      0x1f
  UM def        0      0      0      0      1      0      0x1f

Two different claims follow, and they are worth keeping apart:

  1. The offsets are right and the block is the documented one. Every
     register with a non-zero documented default reads exactly that
     default on a board that has not touched it (the second 6 GiB board): WDT_CFG 0x1,
     WDT_OCFG 0x1F. A wrong base would not reproduce two distinct
     documented defaults at two distinct offsets.
  2. The block is live, not a hole. The one register that differs
     between the boards is WDT_MODE: the first 6 GiB board reads 0xb1 = bit0 (enable) +
     bits[7:4] = 0xb (512k cycles = 16 s), i.e. what a watchdog
     userspace on the first 6 GiB board's BSP kernel would arm. Reads only -- nothing was
     written to this block on any board.

Corollary for the clocks: WDT_CFG bit[8] = 0 on both boards, so the
selected source is RTC_32K, the "losc" input.

RESET SCOPE -- still relevant, now only for /dev/watchdog

`sun55i_wdt_reg` sets `wdt_reset_mask = 0x03, wdt_reset_val = 0x01`, so
`sunxi_wdt_restart()` writes WDT_CFG = 0x16AA0001, byte-for-byte the
value the UM's own example in 8.3.4.3 writes to WDT_CFG_REG for a whole-system
reset. 8.3.1 describes the watchdog as the block that issues a
system-wide reset after a fault.

That this actually resets this SoC is **observed**, not merely read:
2026-07-27 on the 8 GiB board, on the blob chain and with the then-still-present
priority carry, `reboot -f` printed `reboot: Restarting system` and
`[74]HELLO! BOOT0 is starting!` followed immediately, through to a full
boot. So the kernel-side watchdog
restart path works on this silicon. It is simply no longer the path
`reboot` takes, because BL31 now services SYSTEM_RESET itself.

What the UM still does not settle, unchanged and unclosed: what "whole
system" excludes (AON/PRCM, RTC, E902, the WDT block itself are all
plausible and none is named), and whether eMMC RST_n is driven -- which
the eMMC-boot work inherits.

THE CLOCK REFERENCE

The node uses `clocks = <&osc24M>, <&osc32k>`. Since the CPU-DVFS series
landed, `arm64-dts-a733-model-the-26mhz-dcxo` renames that node to `sys24M-clk` and keeps **both**
labels on it (`sys24M: osc24M: sys24M-clk`), so `&osc24M` and `&sys24M`
resolve to the same clock and the choice is cosmetic today. The
substantive point is the one the DTS comment makes: neither label is the
physically correct source. UM 8.3.3 lists CLK_32K and the DCXO divided by
750 (26 MHz / 750) as the WDT sources, so the high-frequency
input is the **26 MHz DCXO**, not the 24 MHz SYS_CLK24M either label
names. `sunxi_wdt` never calls `clk_get()`, so no rate is consumed and
nothing computes on the wrong number, and the source actually selected
on silicon is the losc one, which the node describes correctly. The
follow-up stands: re-point "hosc" at the RTC-CCU's DCXO.

BINDING

`allwinner,sun4i-a10-wdt.yaml` has `unevaluatedProperties: false` and
lists `allwinner,sun55i-a523-wdt` only as a bare `const`, so the
two-item `allwinner,sun60i-a733-wdt` -> `allwinner,sun55i-a523-wdt`
compatible has to be added or `dtbs_check` rejects the node. The
existing `if:` clause matches with `contains:`, so the A733 keeps the
sun55i clock schema (hosc/losc + required `clock-names`) automatically.

`SUNXI_WATCHDOG` is pinned =y in the recipe's kernel config, which
is what makes the driver present at all on a bring-up initramfs; the
arm64 defconfig ships it `=m`.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED

Verified:

  * The node's effect on silicon: see RESET SCOPE above, the 8 GiB board,
    2026-07-27.
  * The two silicon dumps quoted above were taken on the first and the
    second 6 GiB board, on 2026-07-22, read-only.
  * This revision was produced mechanically, not by hand-editing hunks:
    the series was applied to a freshly unpacked 6.18.36 up to the CPU OPP patch, the
    carry was removed in the resulting tree, and the diff regenerated
    against that base.

Not verified by this revision:

  * The tree has not been booted since the carry was removed. What that
    would show is *nothing new about the watchdog* -- `reboot` no longer
    goes through it either way -- so the observable that matters is the
    narrow one: with this patch the board must still `reboot` (BL31's
    own path) and `/dev/watchdog` must still appear. Both are cheap to
    read off the next board session that happens for another reason.
  * `dtbs_check` has not been run against the amended binding (no
    dt-schema in this environment).

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/watchdog/allwinner,sun4i-a10-wdt.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/watchdog/allwinner,sun4i-a10-wdt.yaml
@@ -30,12 +30,15 @@
           - const: allwinner,sun6i-a31-wdt
       - const: allwinner,sun20i-d1-wdt
       - items:
           - const: allwinner,sun20i-d1-wdt-reset
           - const: allwinner,sun20i-d1-wdt
       - const: allwinner,sun55i-a523-wdt
+      - items:
+          - const: allwinner,sun60i-a733-wdt
+          - const: allwinner,sun55i-a523-wdt
 
   reg:
     maxItems: 1
 
   clocks:
     minItems: 1
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -543,12 +543,47 @@
 			 */
 			cpu_speed_grade: cpu-speed-grade@4c {
 				reg = <0x4c 4>;
 			};
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * WDT0_CPUX. Real hardware, described unconditionally. The
+		 * `priority` carry that used to sit in this node is gone: BL31
+		 * is built from source now and resets over its own
+		 * watchdog, so nothing has to outrank PSCI any more.
+		 *
+		 * reg    A733 UM 8.3.5 register list: WDT0_CPUX @ 0x02050000
+		 *        (4 KiB block in the memory map). 0x20
+		 *        spans the whole documented register set (the last one
+		 *        is WDT_OCFG_REG at +0x18), same as sun55i-a523.dtsi.
+		 * irq    A733 UM Table 12-1, INTID 138 "WDT0" -> GIC SPI
+		 *        138 - 32 = 106.
+		 *        Deliberately NOT the A523's SPI 63: the two SoCs share
+		 *        the register layout, not the interrupt map.
+		 * clocks UM 8.3.3: the WDT's two selectable sources are RTC_32K
+		 *        and DCXO/750, picked by WDT_CFG_REG bit[8]. "losc" is
+		 *        therefore exactly right, and it is the selected one on
+		 *        silicon (WDT_CFG bit8 = 0 on both 6 GiB boards, 2026-07-22).
+		 *        "hosc" is the closest approximation the DT can express
+		 *        today: the real high-frequency source is the 26 MHz
+		 *        DCXO, not the 24 MHz SYS_CLK24M this node names, and
+		 *        no DCXO clock provider exists on this branch. The
+		 *        driver reads neither rate (sunxi_wdt never calls
+		 *        clk_get), so nothing computes on it -- but re-point
+		 *        "hosc" at the RTC-CCU's DCXO once that lands.
+		 */
+		wdt: watchdog@2050000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-wdt",
+				     "allwinner,sun55i-a523-wdt";
+			reg = <0x0 0x02050000 0x0 0x20>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 106 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&osc24M>, <&osc32k>;
+			clock-names = "hosc", "losc";
+		};
+
 		gic: interrupt-controller@3400000 {
 			compatible = "arm,gic-v3";
 			#interrupt-cells = <3>;
 			interrupt-controller;
 			reg = <0x0 0x03400000 0x0 0x10000>,	/* GIC Dist */
 			      <0x0 0x03460000 0x0 0x100000>;	/* GIC Redist */
